// T4 map release, the POSITIVE case: a map local's ENTRY NODES are reclaimed.
//
// A map is a bare pointer at the slot (its LLVM form is an opaque pointer), so
// codegen_arc_note_local records it with field == -1 and the release at exit
// frees the GooMapSV struct. That is ONE block. Every GooMapEntrySV hanging off
// `m->head` used to leak, because goo_release ends in goo_free(ptr) and the ARC
// header carries no type tag -- nothing in the release path could walk what an
// object CONTAINS.
//
// Measured on bench/daemon (docs/adr/0002-measurements/element_scan_spike.md):
// the `counts` map is 40.8% of everything the daemon still loses, and 822,000 of
// its 902,000 bytes per 2,000 requests are exactly these entry nodes.
//
// THE FIX IS A DESTRUCTOR, not a bigger free. goo_release_with(ptr, dtor) runs
// `dtor` when -- and only when -- the release takes the count to 0, and
// goo_map_dtor walks the entry chain. The entry walk itself is not new:
// goo_map_clear_sv already does it, and already applies the key rule below.
//
// KEYS ARE NOT FREED, and that is the whole ownership story. goo_map_set_sv
// stores the caller's key pointer VERBATIM, so the map never owns key storage --
// a key can be a string literal's constant data. goo_map_delete_sv records this
// rule and goo_map_clear_sv applies it. examples/arc_release_mapkey_probe.goo is
// the negative case that pins it.
//
// TWO LITERAL KEYS, and a plain `counts["a"] = n`. NOT the daemon's
// `counts[f] = counts[f] + 1`, which is REFUSED: the compound update marks the
// map escaping on account of its own contents. That refusal is a separate
// increment, and using its shape here would make this probe measure nothing.
//
// IMPORT-FREE, for the reason the other arc probes record.
package main

var sink int

func tally(n int) {
	counts := map[string]int{}
	counts["a"] = n
	counts["b"] = n + 1
	sink = sink + len(counts)
}

func main() {
	for i := 0; i < 10000; i++ {
		tally(i)
	}
}
